##  3. LARGER OR SMALLER  (Var(B1hat), sd, standard errors)
##  Var(B1hat) = sigma^2 / SST_X (simple)     Var(Bjhat) = sigma^2 / (SST_j (1 - R_j^2)) (multiple)
##
##   makes Var / sd / se LARGER          |  makes Var / sd / se SMALLER
##   ------------------------------------|-------------------------------------
##   larger error variance sigma^2       |  smaller error variance sigma^2
##   SMALLER sample                      |  LARGER sample
##   X bunched together (smaller SST_X)  |  X more spread out (larger SST_X)
##   LARGER R_j^2 (X_j highly correlated |  smaller R_j^2 (X_j less related to
##     with the other x's)               |     the other x's)
##   NEVER MATTERS: size of the true slope B1 / Bj, the intercept B0
##########################################################################

# [Quiz 8 #8] Holding everything else fixed, which change makes Var(B1hat) larger?
#   - A larger sample.
#   - A larger error variance sigma^2.
#   - More spread-out values of X (a larger SST_X).
#   - A larger slope B1.
#   ANSWER: A larger error variance sigma^2.
#   FLIP (smaller): A larger sample / More spread-out values of X.

# [PS9 #13] Holding everything else fixed, which change makes Var(B1hat) smaller?
#   - A larger intercept B0.
#   - A larger error variance sigma^2.
#   - A smaller sample.
#   - More spread-out values of X (a larger SST_X).
#   ANSWER: More spread-out values of X (a larger SST_X).
#   FLIP (larger): A larger error variance sigma^2 / A smaller sample.

# [Quiz 9 #5] Under SLR1-SLR5, and holding everything else fixed, which changes would make Var(B1hat) smaller? Select all that apply.
#   - A smaller error variance sigma^2
#   - More spread-out X values (a larger SST_X)
#   - A larger error variance sigma^2
#   - X values bunched more tightly together (a smaller SST_X)
#   - A larger true slope B1
#   ANSWER: A smaller error variance sigma^2  AND  More spread-out X values (a larger SST_X)
#   FLIP (larger): A larger error variance sigma^2  AND  X values bunched more tightly together

# [PS10 #6] Under SLR1-SLR5, Var(B1hat) = sigma^2 / SST_X. Holding everything else fixed, which changes would make Var(B1hat) larger? Select all that apply.
#   - A smaller sample (fewer observations with X spread the same way, so a smaller SST_X)
#   - A larger true slope B1
#   - A larger error variance sigma^2
#   - X values bunched more tightly together (a smaller SST_X)
#   - A larger sample (more observations with X spread the same way, so a larger SST_X)
#   ANSWER: A smaller sample  AND  A larger error variance sigma^2  AND  X values bunched more tightly together
#   FLIP (smaller): A larger sample (and a smaller sigma^2, more spread-out X)

# [MOCK EXAM #9] Holding everything else fixed, which make se(Bjhat) LARGER? Select all that apply.
#   - A. A larger error variance sigma^2
#   - B. A larger sample
#   - C. X_j more highly correlated with the other x's (larger R_j^2)
#   - D. A larger true Bj
#   - E. Less variation in X_j (smaller SST_j)
#   ANSWER: A, C, E      (B makes it SMALLER; D never matters)
#   FLIP (SMALLER): a smaller sigma^2, a LARGER sample, X_j LESS correlated with the others (smaller R_j^2), MORE variation in X_j (larger SST_j)

# [PS12 #7] Holding sigma-hat and SST_j fixed, what happens to se(Bjhat) as R_j^2 gets close to 1
# (that is, as X_j becomes nearly a linear function of the other independent variables)?
#   - It shrinks toward 0.
#   - It does not change.
#   - It approaches sigma-hat.
#   - It grows without bound (multicollinearity).
#   ANSWER: It grows without bound (multicollinearity).
#   FLIP (R_j^2 = 0): se = sigma-hat / sqrt(SST_j), the simple-regression formula.

# (pattern) Sample k times as large (X spread the same way): sd(B1hat) is DIVIDED by sqrt(k) (not by k). Var divided by k.
# (pattern) sigma doubles: sd(B1hat) doubles, Var times 4.

# [PS11 #10] Unbiasedness only needs MLR1-MLR4. Why not simply add every variable we can find to the regression?
#   - Adding any variable always makes the other estimates biased.
#   - R cannot estimate a regression with more than three independent variables.
#   - Adding variables can increase the variance of the OLS estimates and can even violate MLR3.
#   - Adding a variable always lowers R^2.
#   ANSWER: Adding variables can increase the variance of the OLS estimates and can even violate MLR3.


