##  4. SLR / MLR ASSUMPTIONS
##  MLR1 linear in parameters | MLR2 random sampling | MLR3 no perfect collinearity
##  MLR4 zero conditional mean E[U|X] = 0 (fails -> endogenous, BIASED) | MLR5 homoskedasticity
##########################################################################

# [PS8 #1] In test score = B0 + B1 class size + U, family income is part of U. Richer school districts tend to have smaller classes.
# Which statement is correct?
#   - The zero conditional mean assumption E[U | class size] = 0 holds, because family income is not in the model.
#   - B1 must equal zero.
#   - U is always equal to zero.
#   - The zero conditional mean assumption fails, because the average of U changes with class size.
#   ANSWER: The zero conditional mean assumption fails, because the average of U changes with class size.

# [Exam I #5] In yield = B0 + B1 fertilizer + U, land quality is part of U. Suppose farmers put more fertilizer on better-quality land. Then:
#   - the zero conditional mean assumption fails, because the average of U changes with fertilizer
#   - U is always equal to zero
#   - B1 must equal zero
#   - the zero conditional mean assumption E[U | fertilizer] = 0 still holds
#   ANSWER: the zero conditional mean assumption fails, because the average of U changes with fertilizer

# [Quiz 10 #7] In wage = B0 + B1 educ + U, ability is part of the error U. Ability raises wages and is positively correlated with education.
# Which assumption fails?
#   - MLR2 (random sampling)
#   - MLR4 (E[U | educ] = 0), so educ is endogenous
#   - MLR3 (no perfect collinearity)
#   - MLR1 (the population model can be written as Y = B0 + B1 X1 + ... + BK XK + U)
#   ANSWER: MLR4 (E[U | educ] = 0), so educ is endogenous

# [PS11 #9] Which situation makes MLR4, E[U | X1, ..., XK] = 0, fail (so that the X variables are endogenous)?
#   - X1 and X2 are highly, but not perfectly, correlated.
#   - A variable that affects Y and is correlated with X1 is left out of the regression.
#   - The sample has only 50 observations.
#   - Y is measured in thousands of dollars instead of dollars.
#   ANSWER: A variable that affects Y and is correlated with X1 is left out of the regression.   (or wrong functional form)

# [PS9 #10] In a sample of workers, every worker has exactly 12 years of education. You regress wage on education. What goes wrong?
#   - R^2 must equal 1.
#   - SLR3 fails: the X values are all the same, so the OLS slope cannot be computed.
#   - SLR4 fails, so the slope is biased.
#   - Nothing: OLS works as usual.
#   ANSWER: SLR3 fails: the X values are all the same, so the OLS slope cannot be computed.

# [PS9 #17] In a regression of food spending on income, the spread of food spending around the regression line grows as income grows.
# Which statement is correct?
#   - SLR3 fails, so the OLS slope cannot be computed.
#   - SLR4 fails, so the OLS slope is biased.
#   - All five SLR assumptions still hold.
#   - SLR5 (homoskedasticity) fails, so the variance formula of Theorem 2.2, Var(B1hat) = sigma^2/SST_X, no longer applies (OLS is still unbiased).
#   ANSWER: SLR5 (homoskedasticity) fails, so the variance formula ... no longer applies (OLS is still unbiased).

# [PS11 #7] Which of these regressions violate MLR3 (no perfect collinearity), so that OLS cannot estimate them? Select all that apply.
#   - a regression with 5 independent variables, estimated on 4 observations            <- VIOLATES
#   - wage on educ and exper in a sample of 526 workers, where both educ and exper vary from worker to worker
#   - wage on income in dollars and income in thousands of dollars                       <- VIOLATES
#   - colGPA on ACT and hsGPA, which are positively (but not perfectly) correlated
#   - voteA on expendA, expendB and totexpend, where totexpend = expendA + expendB       <- VIOLATES
#   ANSWER: 5 variables on 4 observations; income in dollars AND thousands; voteA with totexpend = expendA + expendB

# [Quiz 10 #6] Which of these regressions violate MLR3 (no perfect collinearity), so that OLS cannot estimate them? Select all that apply.
#   - profit on online sales, store sales and total sales, where total sales = online sales + store sales   <- VIOLATES
#   - test score on study hours and sleep hours, which are negatively (but not perfectly) correlated
#   - a regression with 3 independent variables, estimated on 3 observations                                <- VIOLATES
#   - wage on educ alone
#   - house price on size in square feet and size in square meters (computed from the square feet)          <- VIOLATES
#   ANSWER: total sales = online + store; 3 variables on 3 observations; square feet AND square meters
#   (also VIOLATES: exper = age - educ - 6 with all three; temperature in F and C; an x that never varies)
#   (OK: x and x^2, x and log(x) - not LINEAR functions; any correlation that is not perfect)

# [PS11 #8] Data: vote1. Create totexpend <- expendA + expendB, then run lm(voteA ~ expendA + expendB + totexpend).
# What does R report for the coefficient on totexpend?
#   - A very large number, because totexpend is highly correlated with expendA.
#   - NA: R drops totexpend because it is an exact linear function of expendA and expendB.
#   - Exactly 0, because totexpend adds no new information.
#   - The sum of the expendA and expendB coefficients.
#   ANSWER: NA: R drops totexpend because it is an exact linear function of expendA and expendB.

# Gauss-Markov: under MLR1-MLR5, OLS is BLUE = Best Linear Unbiased Estimator.
#   "Best" = SMALLEST VARIANCE among linear unbiased estimators.   (not "unbiased", not "highest R^2")


